        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why won’t my Weslo treadmill turn on?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check three things in order: the key is fully inserted, the power switch on the frame near the cord has not tripped (if it protrudes, wait five minutes and press it back in), and the treadmill is on a surge suppressor in a properly grounded outlet. Weslo’s manuals say the treadmill is not compatible with GFCI-equipped outlets.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I reset a Weslo treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Unplug the power cord, wait five minutes, plug it back in, then remove and reinsert the key. If the power switch beside the cord has tripped, wait five minutes and press it back in; the Cadence 1000 manual also asks you to move the key to off first. That is the reset Weslo’s manuals describe.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Where is the reset button on a Weslo treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On the treadmill frame near the power cord. It is a power switch that also works as a circuit breaker: if it sticks out, it has tripped. Wait five minutes, then press it back in.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I tighten the belt on a Weslo treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Remove the key and unplug. With the supplied hex key, turn both rear idler roller screws clockwise a quarter turn, keeping the belt centred. It is right when you can lift each edge of the belt 2 to 3 inches off the deck. Walk on it for a few minutes and repeat if it still slips.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I lubricate my Weslo treadmill belt?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not unless an authorised service representative tells you to. Weslo’s manual says the belt is coated with high-performance lubricant and warns never to apply silicone spray or other substances to the belt or deck, because they can deteriorate the belt and cause excessive wear.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I still get parts for a Weslo treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often, yes. Find the model and serial numbers (front cover of the manual and the serial decal on the frame) and the part’s key number from the manual’s parts list, then ask iFIT Health and Fitness, Weslo’s parent company, or search third-party parts sellers by the part number.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
